1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,319
Why does a five-percent salary raise

2
00:00:02,319 --> 00:00:04,895
during six-percent inflation feel like a

3
00:00:04,895 --> 00:00:05,410
victory,

4
00:00:05,410 --> 00:00:07,857
even though your real purchasing power

5
00:00:07,857 --> 00:00:08,630
just shrank?

6
00:00:08,630 --> 00:00:11,284
Economist Irving Fisher named this Money

7
00:00:11,284 --> 00:00:12,412
Illusion in 1928.

8
00:00:12,412 --> 00:00:12,943
In 1997,

9
00:00:12,943 --> 00:00:13,805
Eldar Shafir,

10
00:00:13,805 --> 00:00:14,734
Peter Diamond,

11
00:00:14,734 --> 00:00:16,725
and Amos Tversky proved in the

12
00:00:16,725 --> 00:00:19,512
Quarterly Journal of Economics that people

13
00:00:19,512 --> 00:00:22,630
celebrate bigger nominal raises despite smaller

14
00:00:22,630 --> 00:00:23,360
real gains.

15
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:25,830
Watching raw dollar numbers tricks savers

16
00:00:25,830 --> 00:00:28,239
into leaving large balances in near-zero

17
00:00:28,239 --> 00:00:29,323
checking accounts,

18
00:00:29,323 --> 00:00:31,251
smiling at a steady number while

19
00:00:31,251 --> 00:00:33,540
inflation quietly erodes buying power.

20
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:35,282
Think strictly in real terms:

21
00:00:35,282 --> 00:00:37,804
subtract inflation from every salary bump,

22
00:00:37,804 --> 00:00:38,645
savings yield,

23
00:00:38,645 --> 00:00:41,348
and investment return before deciding whether

24
00:00:41,348 --> 00:00:43,510
your household actually moved ahead.

25
00:00:43,510 --> 00:00:46,311
Track real purchasing power after inflation,

26
00:00:46,311 --> 00:00:48,666
never flattering nominal numbers on a

27
00:00:48,666 --> 00:00:49,430
bank screen.
